"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[19764],{919764:n=>{n.exports="# SQL Functions — Date & Time\n\nSnowflake date/time functions → DBSQL equivalents. Includes format-string mapping and drift warnings.\n\n## SNOWFLAKE\n\n### Format-string token mapping (CRITICAL)\n\nSnowflake format strings use Oracle-style tokens with `MI` for minutes. DBSQL uses Java `DateTimeFormatter` patterns with lowercase `mm` for minutes — and `MM` in DBSQL means **month**. This is a silent wrong-output risk when translating naively.\n\n| Snowflake token | DBSQL `DateTimeFormatter` | Meaning                                                                                                           |\n| --------------- | ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `YYYY`          | `yyyy`                    | 4-digit year                                                                                                      |\n| `YY`            | `yy`                      | 2-digit year                                                                                                      |\n| `MM`            | `MM`                      | 2-digit month (same token!)                                                                                       |\n| `MON`           | `MMM`                     | 3-letter month name                                                                                               |\n| `MMMM`          | `MMMM`                    | Full month name                                                                                                   |\n| `DD`            | `dd`                      | Day of month                                                                                                      |\n| `DY`            | `EEE`                     | 3-letter day name                                                                                                 |\n| `DAY`           | `EEEE`                    | Full day name                                                                                                     |\n| `HH24` / `HH`   | `HH`                      | Hour (24-hour)                                                                                                    |\n| `HH12`          | `hh`                      | Hour (12-hour)                                                                                                    |\n| `AM` / `PM`     | `a`                       | AM/PM marker                                                                                                      |\n| `MI`            | `mm`                      | **Minute — NOT `MM`**                                                                                             |\n| `SS`            | `ss`                      | Second                                                                                                            |\n| `FF3`           | `SSS`                     | Milliseconds (3 digits)                                                                                           |\n| `FF6`           | `SSSSSS`                  | Microseconds (6 digits)                                                                                           |\n| `FF` / `FF9`    | `SSSSSSSSS`               | Nanoseconds pattern — but Spark truncates to microsecond (6-digit) precision on parse; the last 3 digits are lost |\n| `TZH:TZM`       | `XXX`                     | Timezone offset                                                                                                   |\n\n**Worked examples:**\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nSELECT TO_DATE('2024-03-20', 'YYYY-MM-DD');\nSELECT TO_TIMESTAMP('2024-03-20 14:35:00', 'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS');\nSELECT TO_CHAR(CURRENT_DATE, 'YYYYMMDD');\nSELECT TO_CHAR(CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, 'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS');\n\n-- DBSQL\nSELECT TO_DATE('2024-03-20', 'yyyy-MM-dd');\nSELECT TO_TIMESTAMP('2024-03-20 14:35:00', 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss');\nSELECT DATE_FORMAT(CURRENT_DATE, 'yyyyMMdd');\nSELECT DATE_FORMAT(CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss');\n```\n\nThe most dangerous silent bug: using `MM` for minutes in DBSQL (it's month) produces plausible-looking wrong timestamps.\n\n### Date function mapping\n\n| Snowflake                                                                        | DBSQL                                                                                                   | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CURRENT_DATE()` / `CURRENT_DATE`                                                | `CURRENT_DATE()` / `CURRENT_DATE`                                                                       | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()` / `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`                                      | `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()` / `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`                                                             | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `SYSDATE()`                                                                      | `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()` — but Snowflake SYSDATE returns UTC whereas CURRENT_TIMESTAMP respects session TZ | For UTC: `UTC_TIMESTAMP()` or `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `LOCALTIMESTAMP()`                                                               | `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()`                                                                                   |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `LOCALTIME()` / `CURRENT_TIME()`                                                 | DBSQL has no TIME type — cast via `DATE_FORMAT(CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, 'HH:mm:ss')`                          |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEADD(unit, n, date)`                                                         | `DATEADD(unit, n, date)`                                                                                | **DBSQL supports 3-arg DATEADD** (DBR 10.4+) with the same argument order as Snowflake. Alternatives: `DATE_ADD(date, n)` for days, `ADD_MONTHS(date, n)` for months, interval arithmetic `ts + INTERVAL n HOURS`.                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| `DATEDIFF(unit, d1, d2)`                                                         | `DATEDIFF(unit, d1, d2)`                                                                                | **Silent drift — boundary-crossing vs elapsed-unit.** Snowflake counts calendar-boundary crossings; DBSQL counts whole elapsed units. Example: `DATEDIFF(MONTH, '2021-01-31', '2021-02-01')` = 1 in Snowflake, 0 in DBSQL. See drift section below.                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `DATEDIFF('day', d1, d2)`                                                        | `DATEDIFF(DAY, d1, d2)` or `DATEDIFF(d2, d1)` (2-arg, days only)                                        | Day-level no drift (integer days). 2-arg DBSQL form has reversed order.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| `DATE_TRUNC(unit, date)`                                                         | `DATE_TRUNC(unit, date)` or `TRUNC(date, unit)`                                                         | Both support the same unit names: `'YEAR'`, `'QUARTER'`, `'MONTH'`, `'WEEK'`, `'DAY'`, `'HOUR'`, `'MINUTE'`, `'SECOND'`, `'MILLISECOND'`, `'MICROSECOND'`. DBSQL also accepts aliases (`YYYY`, `MM`, `DD`, `MON`).                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| `DATE_PART(unit, date)` / `DATEPART(unit, date)`                                 | `DATE_PART(unit, date)` or `EXTRACT(unit FROM date)`                                                    | Same unit names                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `YEAR(date)`, `MONTH(date)`, `DAY(date)`, `HOUR(ts)`, `MINUTE(ts)`, `SECOND(ts)` | Same                                                                                                    | Safe drop-in                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `QUARTER(date)`                                                                  | `QUARTER(date)`                                                                                         | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `WEEK(date)` / `WEEKOFYEAR(date)`                                                | `WEEKOFYEAR(date)`                                                                                      | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `DAYOFWEEK(date)` / `DAYOFWEEK_ISO(date)`                                        | `DAYOFWEEK(date)`                                                                                       | **Silent drift.** Snowflake default (`WEEK_START = 0`) returns `0..6` (0=Sunday). DBSQL always returns `1..7` (1=Sunday). Always off by one. Rewrite as `DAYOFWEEK(date) - 1` to match Snowflake. For `DAYOFWEEK_ISO` (1=Mon..7=Sun), compute via `((DAYOFWEEK(date) + 5) % 7) + 1`.                                                                                                                            |\n| `DAYOFMONTH(date)`                                                               | `DAY(date)` / `DAYOFMONTH(date)`                                                                        | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `DAYOFYEAR(date)`                                                                | `DAYOFYEAR(date)`                                                                                       | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `DAYNAME(date)`                                                                  | `DATE_FORMAT(date, 'EEE')`                                                                              | 3-letter day name                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| `MONTHNAME(date)`                                                                | `DATE_FORMAT(date, 'MMM')`                                                                              | 3-letter month name                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `LAST_DAY(date)`                                                                 | `LAST_DAY(date)`                                                                                        | 1-arg: same (last day of month)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `LAST_DAY(date, 'part')`                                                         | `DATE_TRUNC('part', date)` + interval arithmetic                                                        | **DBSQL `LAST_DAY` is 1-arg only — `LAST_DAY(date, 'QUARTER')` is a parse error in DBSQL.** Snowflake 2-arg `LAST_DAY(date, 'MONTH'/'QUARTER'/'YEAR'/'WEEK')` must be rewritten. `MONTH` collapses to bare `LAST_DAY(date)`. For `QUARTER`/`YEAR`: `DATE_SUB(DATE_TRUNC('QUARTER', date) + INTERVAL 3 MONTH, 1)` (and `12 MONTH` for year). `'WEEK'` depends on Snowflake `WEEK_START` and needs matching math. |\n| `NEXT_DAY(date, 'Monday')`                                                       | `NEXT_DAY(date, 'Monday')`                                                                              | DBSQL accepts full, 3-letter, or 2-letter day names (case-insensitive) — no rewrite needed                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `PREVIOUS_DAY(date, 'Mon')`                                                      | No direct equivalent — rewrite with arithmetic                                                          |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `ADD_MONTHS(date, n)`                                                            | `ADD_MONTHS(date, n)`                                                                                   | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `MONTHS_BETWEEN(d1, d2)`                                                         | `MONTHS_BETWEEN(d1, d2)`                                                                                | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `TIMEADD(unit, n, ts)` / `TIMESTAMPADD(unit, n, ts)`                             | `DATEADD(unit, n, ts)` or interval arithmetic                                                           |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `TIMEDIFF(unit, ts1, ts2)` / `TIMESTAMPDIFF(unit, ts1, ts2)`                     | `TIMESTAMPDIFF(unit, ts1, ts2)` or `DATEDIFF(unit, ts1, ts2)`                                           | Same arg order; **same elapsed-vs-boundary drift as `DATEDIFF`** — see drift section below.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| `CONVERT_TIMEZONE('src_tz', 'tgt_tz', ts)` (3-arg)                               | `FROM_UTC_TIMESTAMP(TO_UTC_TIMESTAMP(ts, 'src_tz'), 'tgt_tz')`                                          | Generic: lift to UTC, then convert. Collapse to `FROM_UTC_TIMESTAMP(ts, 'tgt_tz')` only when `src_tz = 'UTC'`. Snowflake 3-arg returns `TIMESTAMP_NTZ`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| `CONVERT_TIMEZONE('tgt_tz', ts)` (2-arg)                                         | `FROM_UTC_TIMESTAMP(ts, 'tgt_tz')` or session-TZ display                                                | Snowflake 2-arg returns `TIMESTAMP_TZ` (per-value offset). DBSQL `TIMESTAMP` has no per-value offset — offset is lost.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `DATE_FROM_PARTS(y, m, d)`                                                       | `MAKE_DATE(y, m, d)`                                                                                    |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `TIME_FROM_PARTS(h, m, s)`                                                       | No TIME type — construct a STRING via `DATE_FORMAT`                                                     |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `TIMESTAMP_FROM_PARTS(y, mo, d, h, mi, s)`                                       | `MAKE_TIMESTAMP(y, mo, d, h, mi, s)`                                                                    |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `TO_DATE(str [, fmt])`                                                           | `TO_DATE(str [, fmt])`                                                                                  | Format tokens differ (see table above)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `TO_TIMESTAMP(str [, fmt])`                                                      | `TO_TIMESTAMP(str [, fmt])`                                                                             | Format tokens differ                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(str [, fmt])`                                                  | `TO_TIMESTAMP(str [, fmt])` cast to `TIMESTAMP_NTZ` (DBR 13.3 LTS+)                                     |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(str)`                                                          | `TO_TIMESTAMP(str)`                                                                                     | DBSQL `TIMESTAMP` is LTZ semantics                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| `TO_TIMESTAMP_TZ(str)`                                                           | `TO_TIMESTAMP(str)` — offset lost                                                                       | Flag if source relies on per-value offset                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| `TO_CHAR(date, 'fmt')`                                                           | `DATE_FORMAT(date, 'fmt')`                                                                              | Format tokens differ                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `TRY_TO_DATE(str)`                                                               | `TRY_CAST(str AS DATE)` or `TRY_TO_DATE(str)`                                                           |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `TRY_TO_TIMESTAMP(str)`                                                          | `TRY_CAST(str AS TIMESTAMP)` or `TRY_TO_TIMESTAMP(str)`                                                 |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATE(ts)` (Snowflake cast-like function)                                        | `CAST(ts AS DATE)` or `TO_DATE(ts)`                                                                     |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `EXTRACT(unit FROM date)`                                                        | `EXTRACT(unit FROM date)`                                                                               | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n\n### INTERVAL literal — preserve the unit (CRITICAL)\n\nSnowflake accepts `INTERVAL '672 hours'` (number and unit inside one quoted string). DBSQL does **not** parse that form — the value and unit must be separate. Valid DBSQL forms:\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL — bare numeric + unit keyword (singular or plural)\nINTERVAL 672 HOURS\nINTERVAL 2 DAY\nINTERVAL 30 MINUTES\n\n-- DBSQL — quoted multi-units form (year-month OR day-time, never both)\nINTERVAL '2 YEARS 3 MONTHS'\nINTERVAL '5 DAYS 4 HOURS'\n\n-- DBSQL — ANSI qualifier form (year-month OR day-time, never both)\nINTERVAL '100-00' YEAR TO MONTH\nINTERVAL '3 12:00:00' DAY TO SECOND\n\n-- INVALID in DBSQL — parses in Snowflake but not here\nINTERVAL '672 hours'      -- Snowflake form, NOT DBSQL\nINTERVAL 672              -- missing unit — silently nonsensical\nINTERVAL '1 YEAR 3 DAYS'  -- mixes year-month with day-time — Spark rejects\n```\n\nTranslate `INTERVAL '672 hours'` → `INTERVAL 672 HOURS`. Never strip the unit: `INTERVAL 672` is either a parse error or — when it slips past parsing in some context — a unitless number that breaks downstream date arithmetic. Apply the same rule to `'N days'`, `'N minutes'`, `'N seconds'`, `'N months'`, `'N years'`.\n\n**Year-month and day-time do not mix in a single literal.** Spark grammar disallows mixing `YEAR[S]`/`MONTH[S]` with `WEEK[S]`/`DAY[S]`/`HOUR[S]`/`MINUTE[S]`/`SECOND[S]` in _both_ the multi-units form and the ANSI qualifier form (no `YEAR TO HOUR`). If the Snowflake source needed both, emit two separate intervals and add them: `ts + INTERVAL '1' YEAR + INTERVAL '3' DAY`.\n\n### DATEDIFF / TIMESTAMPDIFF — boundary-crossing vs elapsed-unit (CRITICAL)\n\nSnowflake `DATEDIFF(unit, d1, d2)` counts **boundary crossings** — the number of calendar-unit boundaries between `d1` and `d2`. DBSQL 3-arg `DATEDIFF` / `TIMESTAMPDIFF` count **whole elapsed units** — a unit is considered elapsed only when the day-and-time is at or past the start.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: 1 (Jan→Feb month boundary crossed)\nSELECT DATEDIFF(MONTH, '2021-01-31', '2021-02-01');\n\n-- DBSQL: 0 (one full month has NOT elapsed — Feb 1 < Jan 31 day-of-month)\nSELECT DATEDIFF(MONTH, '2021-01-31', '2021-02-01');\n\n-- DBSQL: exact boundary-crossing equivalents\nYEAR(d2) - YEAR(d1)                                       -- YEAR boundaries\n(YEAR(d2) * 12 + MONTH(d2)) - (YEAR(d1) * 12 + MONTH(d1)) -- MONTH boundaries\n(YEAR(d2) * 4 + QUARTER(d2)) - (YEAR(d1) * 4 + QUARTER(d1)) -- QUARTER boundaries\n```\n\nApplies to `YEAR`, `QUARTER`, `MONTH`, `WEEK` (and to fractional time units when they straddle boundaries). `DAY`, `HOUR`, `MINUTE`, `SECOND` on exact timestamp inputs agree between the two dialects. Always review source `DATEDIFF`/`TIMESTAMPDIFF` calls and pick elapsed-unit or boundary-crossing per call.\n\n### General scalar function mappings\n\nNon-date scalar functions frequently found alongside date code. Included here because there is no separate general-functions reference file.\n\n| Snowflake                                                   | DBSQL                                                            | Drift warning                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `IFF(cond, t, f)`                                           | `IF(cond, t, f)` or `IFF(cond, t, f)`                            | DBSQL supports both                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `DECODE(expr, v1, r1, ..., default)`                        | `CASE expr WHEN v1 THEN r1 ... ELSE default END`                 | DBSQL has `DECODE` but prefer `CASE` for clarity                                                                                                                                               |\n| `NVL(a, b)`                                                 | `COALESCE(a, b)` or `NVL(a, b)`                                  | Same behavior                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| `NVL2(a, b, c)`                                             | `NVL2(a, b, c)`                                                  | Same — DBSQL supports `NVL2` natively                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `IFNULL(a, b)`                                              | `IFNULL(a, b)` or `COALESCE(a, b)`                               | Same                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `ZEROIFNULL(x)`                                             | `COALESCE(x, 0)`                                                 |                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| `NULLIFZERO(x)`                                             | `NULLIF(x, 0)`                                                   |                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| `DIV0(a, b)`                                                | `IF(b = 0, 0, a / b)`                                            | **Silent drift** — `try_divide(a, b)` returns NULL on zero, not 0                                                                                                                              |\n| `DIV0NULL(a, b)`                                            | `try_divide(a, b)`                                               | Exact match                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `EQUAL_NULL(a, b)`                                          | `a IS NOT DISTINCT FROM b` or `equal_null(a, b)` (DBR 11.3 LTS+) | NULL-safe equality                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `GREATEST(a, b, ...)`                                       | `GREATEST(a, b, ...)`                                            | **NULL handling drift**: Snowflake propagates NULL; DBSQL ignores NULLs. Wrap with explicit null-check                                                                                         |\n| `LEAST(a, b, ...)`                                          | `LEAST(a, b, ...)`                                               | Same NULL drift as `GREATEST`                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| `BOOLAND_AGG(col)` / `BOOLOR_AGG(col)` / `BOOLXOR_AGG(col)` | `BOOL_AND(col)` / `BOOL_OR(col)`                                 | DBSQL has bool_and/bool_or; no direct BOOLXOR_AGG                                                                                                                                              |\n| `HASH(col)`                                                 | `HASH(col)` **or** `xxhash64(col)`                               | **Algorithm AND return type differ.** Snowflake `HASH` returns `NUMBER(19,0)` (64-bit signed). DBSQL `HASH` returns `INT` (32-bit, Murmur3). For a stable 64-bit hash, prefer `xxhash64(col)`. |\n| `MD5(str)` / `SHA1(str)` / `SHA2(str, bits)`                | `MD5(str)` / `SHA(str)` / `SHA2(str, bits)`                      | SHA1 is `SHA(...)` in DBSQL. **See critical anti-pattern below.**                                                                                                                              |\n| `UUID_STRING()`                                             | `UUID()`                                                         | v4 UUID                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| `RANDOM()`                                                  | `RAND()` or `RANDOM()`                                           | Both supported in DBSQL                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| `LOG(x)`                                                    | `LOG(x)`                                                         | Natural log                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `LOG(base, x)`                                              | `LOG(base, x)`                                                   | Same order in Snowflake and DBSQL (both use `LOG(base, x)`)                                                                                                                                    |\n| `LOG10(x)`                                                  | `LOG10(x)`                                                       | Same                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `LN(x)`                                                     | `LN(x)`                                                          | Same                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `EXP(x)`                                                    | `EXP(x)`                                                         | Same                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `POWER(x, y)` / `POW(x, y)`                                 | `POWER(x, y)` / `POW(x, y)`                                      | Same                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `SQRT(x)`                                                   | `SQRT(x)`                                                        | Same                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `MOD(a, b)` / `a % b`                                       | `MOD(a, b)` / `a % b`                                            | Sign of result with negative operands may differ — flag if source uses negatives                                                                                                               |\n| `TRUNC(x, scale)`                                           | `TRUNC(x, scale)` (number) or `TRUNC(date, unit)`                | Both overloads supported                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| `ABS(x)` / `SIGN(x)`                                        | `ABS(x)` / `SIGN(x)`                                             | Same                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n\n### MD5 with column concatenation — CRITICAL anti-pattern\n\nSnowflake surrogate-key idiom: hash multiple columns as `VARCHAR` and concatenate them:\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake (common surrogate key pattern)\nSELECT md5(col_a::varchar || '-' || col_b::varchar) AS id\nFROM t;\n```\n\nWhen rewriting `::varchar` → `CAST(... AS STRING)`, the resulting DBSQL must still reference the **columns**, not string literals of the column names:\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL (CORRECT — columns are evaluated)\nSELECT md5(CAST(col_a AS STRING) || '-' || CAST(col_b AS STRING)) AS id\nFROM t;\n\n-- DBSQL (WRONG — wrapping the expression in quotes produces literal strings, not column values)\nSELECT md5(CAST('col_a' AS STRING) || '-' || CAST('col_b' AS STRING)) AS id\nFROM t;\n-- This always hashes the constant string \"col_a-col_b\", regardless of row data.\n```\n\n**Rule**: Inside any `MD5(...)` (or `SHA`, `SHA2`, `xxhash64`) call, column references must remain bare identifiers or qualified names — never wrapped in single quotes. If `col_a` is a column, write `col_a`, not `'col_a'`. Apply the same rule to table-qualified names: `t.col_a`, not `'t.col_a'`.\n"}}]);
